WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.000
Narration should sound like instruction, not like a policy document being read aloud.

00:00:04.000 --> 00:00:08.370
Good narration introduces context, explains relationships, highlights decisions,

00:00:08.370 --> 00:00:09.000
and guides attention.

00:00:09.000 --> 00:00:13.000
It can also create pacing by moving learners from one idea to the next.

00:00:13.000 --> 00:00:14.940
The script should be clear enough to listen to,

00:00:14.940 --> 00:00:17.000
but specific enough to teach something useful.

00:00:17.000 --> 00:00:20.000
Avoid simply duplicating dense on-screen text word for word.

00:00:20.000 --> 00:00:21.945
When learners hear the same text they are trying to read,

00:00:21.945 --> 00:00:24.995
the experience can feel redundant and mentally crowded.

00:00:24.995 --> 00:00:30.040
A better pattern is to keep on-screen text concise and let narration explain the meaning,

00:00:30.040 --> 00:00:30.980
example, consequence, or practical application.

00:00:30.980 --> 00:00:35.000
Captions should remain available so learners can access the spoken content.

00:00:35.000 --> 00:00:36.008
Media should be intentional.

00:00:36.008 --> 00:00:38.940
Voiceover, captions, images, icons, video, diagrams,

00:00:38.940 --> 00:00:41.980
and screen recordings can all improve learning when they support the objective.

00:00:41.980 --> 00:00:43.935
They can also distract when used as filler.

00:00:43.935 --> 00:00:47.000
A diagram may clarify a process better than a paragraph.

00:00:47.000 --> 00:00:48.940
A short video may help with a workflow.

00:00:48.940 --> 00:00:52.000
An icon may help scanning, but it should not replace needed explanation.

00:00:52.000 --> 00:00:55.065
Learner engagement comes from relevance, examples, pacing,

00:00:55.065 --> 00:00:56.995
and application more than gimmicks.

00:00:56.995 --> 00:01:00.510
A course feels engaging when learners see why the topic matters and can connect it to real

00:01:00.510 --> 00:01:01.000
work.

00:01:01.000 --> 00:01:02.935
Keep media organized and replaceable.

00:01:02.935 --> 00:01:04.330
File naming, source tracking,

00:01:04.330 --> 00:01:08.570
and version control at a practical level make future updates easier when policies,

00:01:08.570 --> 00:01:10.950
products, screenshots, or voiceover need to change.

00:01:10.950 --> 00:01:14.000
For narration workflows, keep the script connected to the module structure.

00:01:14.000 --> 00:01:17.000
Label scripts by module and screen, track approved versions,

00:01:17.000 --> 00:01:19.956
and avoid recording before the content is reasonably stable.

00:01:19.956 --> 00:01:21.940
Re-recording is sometimes necessary,

00:01:21.940 --> 00:01:26.180
but disciplined script review prevents unnecessary audio churn and helps captions stay

00:01:26.180 --> 00:01:28.000
aligned with the final spoken message.
